---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import BacklogChart from "../components/BacklogChart.astro";
import {
  ledger, complianceDetail, closures, history, snapshots, fmt, formatDate, inMonths,
} from "../lib/data";
import { registerBySlug, plannedRegisters } from "../lib/registers";
import { FORMS, formHref } from "../lib/forms";

const senate = registerBySlug("senate")!;
const rows = ledger();
const comp = complianceDetail();
const cl = closures();
const hist = history();
const peak = hist.reduce((a, b) => (b.backlog > a.backlog ? b : a));
const observed = hist.filter((p) => p.basis === "observed").at(-1);
const asAt = formatDate(observed?.asAt ?? new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10));

---

<Base
  title="Methods"
  description="What this register counts, where every figure comes from, what is deliberately excluded, and what the method cannot yet see."
>
  <section class="wrap intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methods · as at {asAt}</p>
    <h1>How this is built</h1>
    <p class="lede measure">
      Everything here is drawn from records the Parliament publishes itself. This page
      states what is counted, what is left out and why, and — at the end — what the
      method cannot yet see. Where a judgment is finely balanced, it is resolved in the
      government’s favour, so that every number on this site is a floor rather than a
      ceiling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="unit">What is counted</h2>
    <p>
      The unit is an <strong>obligation</strong>: something a government undertook to
      answer, owed to a body with standing to demand it, by a date that can be
      established from the record. A register is admitted to this site only when all
      three exist — a stated obligation, a claimant, and a date. That is what allows
      this site to report rather than argue.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Senate is the first claimant, not the only one. It has required {senate.rule}{" "}
      since {senate.ruleSince}. A report leaves the register when a response is tabled,
      whatever that response says — the register measures whether an answer was given,
      never whether it was a good one.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Planned registers: {plannedRegisters().map((r) => r.name).join("; ")}.
      Each will be published as its own register with its own rule, not merged into this one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <p>Six, all public:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The President of the Senate’s report</strong> on the status of government
        responses to parliamentary committee reports, tabled twice a year. It lists every
        committee report that requires a response and records, against each, whether a
        response has been received. The authoritative statement of what is owed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Speaker’s schedule</strong> of the status of government responses to
        committee reports, presented to the House at roughly six-monthly intervals. The
        same instrument for the other chamber, and the basis of the House register.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The government’s own status report</strong> on responses to committee
        reports, published for each chamber alongside the presiding officer’s and covering
        the same date. It is where the sentence “the Government’s response is being
        considered” is actually written. It is read to say what the government has said
        about a report — never to add a report to a register or remove one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Senate’s live tracker</strong> of government responses, carried only for
        reports tabled after the President’s report — the window he has not yet covered.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Tabled Documents database</strong> (OTD), through its public API, for
        committee reports and for the text of every government response.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Senate’s own register of responses, 2000 to date</strong>, which supplies
        the compliance record and the dates behind the history chart.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The register follows the President. His report is fetched from the Tabled Documents
      database, not typed in by hand, and its status column decides every row: a date means
      the response has been received and the report leaves the register; a dash means nothing
      has been received; “Interim” means an interim response; and “Interim*” means — in the
      definition his own report gives — that the government has said only that “the
      Government’s response is being considered”. Reports the President records as answered
      are not published here as outstanding, and reports he lists are not filtered out by us.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The House register works the same way</strong>, from the Speaker’s schedule
      rather than the President’s report. Its status column reads slightly differently —
      a date, “no response to date”, “partial response received” or “fully responded”.
      Two further differences matter. The Speaker states whether each report is past its deadline, and
      we take his word for it rather than counting six months off a calendar, because the
      2010 resolution excludes any period when the House was dissolved. And his schedule
      is rolling: a report is dropped from later schedules once its response arrives, so a
      report still listed against an older response date is still owed something, and is
      published here as outstanding — as his own tally counts it. That tally, printed on
      the schedule’s fourth page, is what our reading of the document is checked against
      before the register is written.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tracker cannot add to, subtract from or overrule any of that. It is carried for one
      purpose: reports tabled after the President’s as-at date, which he has not yet reported
      on. Those rows are marked as the tracker’s in the register’s source column, and on many
      builds there are none. Where a response has been tabled since the President’s date, the
      report is removed and the response named. The build refuses to write a file if the
      President’s report comes back short, so a failed fetch cannot quietly shrink the
      register.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Currently {fmt(rows.length)} reports are outstanding, and {fmt(comp.assessable)} responses
      tabled between {comp.firstYear} and {comp.lastYear} can be measured against the deadline.
      A further {fmt(comp.excluded)} cannot: for those responses — almost all of them tabled
      in {comp.excludedYears} — the register does not record when the report was tabled, so no
      deadline can be computed. They are excluded rather than counted as failures. The
      practical effect is that the first two years of the register are barely represented in
      any compliance figure on this site.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="corpus">The response corpus</h2>
    <p>
      Separately from the Ledger, this site examines the text of every government response
      tabled since mid-2022 — {fmt(cl.corpus)} documents — to ask what the response actually did.
      {" "}<strong>Read to {formatDate(cl.readTo)}.</strong> The weekly job downloads and
      classifies whatever has been tabled since it last ran, so this date moves with the
      register rather than with whenever the corpus was last gathered by hand.
      {cl.unreadable > 0 && (
        <>
          {" "}{fmt(cl.unreadable)} document{cl.unreadable === 1 ? "" : "s"} could not be read
          as text. They are counted in the corpus and classified as neither a closure nor a
          substantive response, which understates the closure rate rather than inflating it.
        </>
      )}
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Mid-2022 is not a judgment about when this began. It is where the Tabled Documents
      system starts: it holds no government response tabled before then, so the classification
      cannot reach further back and this site does not claim the practice is new.
    </p>
    <p>
      The database category <em>Government response</em> is broader than that question. It
      also holds documents that answer somebody other than a parliamentary committee, or
      that are not responses at all. {fmt(cl.excluded)} documents are excluded by identifier,
      each with a recorded reason:
    </p>
    <table class="register narrow">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Excluded</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Documents</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {cl.exclusions.map((e) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{e.label}</td>
            <td class="num">{fmt(e.count)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      All but one of these would otherwise have been counted as substantive responses, so
      the exclusions raise the reported failure rate rather than lowering it. The list is
      published in full, with reasons, in the dataset.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="closures">How responses are classified</h2>
    <p>
      One test is applied automatically, and it is a narrow one. A response is a
      <strong> form-letter closure</strong> when it disposes of the committee’s recommendations
      with a repeated template sentence and addresses none of them. Of {fmt(cl.corpus)} responses,
      {" "}<strong>{fmt(cl.proforma)}</strong> are form-letter closures
      ({(cl.rate * 100).toFixed(1)}%), {fmt(cl.partial)} use the template for some
      recommendations but not all, and {fmt(cl.substantive)} do something else.
    </p>
    <p>
      A stratified sample of 56 responses was checked by hand against the source documents,
      deliberately weighted towards the classes most likely to be wrong. That review is what
      produced the scope exclusions above, and the list of undetected forms below.
    </p>
    <h3>What this measure does not catch</h3>
    <p>
      A form-letter closure is only the most conspicuous way of answering without answering.
      There are at least three others. None is detected automatically, and each is named
      here with a document anyone can download and check.
    </p>
    <p>
      They are undetected for one reason, and it is the same reason in all three cases.
      A form-letter closure gives itself away because the template <em>repeats</em>: the same
      sentence appears once per recommendation, and a machine can see that without knowing
      anything about the report. The other three forms are written in ordinary prose that
      varies from document to document, and nothing in the text marks them out. Read on its
      own, a response that says “the Government notes the recommendations of the Committee”
      and then describes a policy is indistinguishable from a real answer — unless you already
      know what the committee recommended, and can check the policy against it.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is the missing ingredient: the response alone is not enough evidence. You cannot
      tell from its own words whether it disposed of five recommendations or forty, or whether
      what it describes answers them. Only the report says what was owed. So these are not
      gaps waiting for a cleverer pattern — a longer list of template phrases would only chase
      the wording of the excuse, and there would be a fifth form next year. They are the same
      gap, and it closes when the reports are parsed as well as the responses.
    </p>
    <ol class="forms">
      {FORMS.map((f) => (
        <li>
          <p class="form-name">
            {f.name}
            <span class={f.detected ? "tag on" : "tag"}>{f.detected ? "detected" : "not detected"}</span>
          </p>
          <p class="form-what">{f.what}</p>
          <p class="note">
            <a href={formHref(f)}>{f.example}</a> — {f.exampleNote}
          </p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <p>
      This is why the published claim is stated narrowly. It is not that
      {" "}{fmt(cl.proforma)} responses answered nothing; it is that {fmt(cl.proforma)} used a
      form-letter closure that addressed no recommendation. The true figure for
      “answered nothing” is higher, and the way to reach it is to ask a better question.
    </p>
    <p>
      That question is <strong>coverage</strong>: for each recommendation a committee made,
      did the response state a position on it? It is countable, it is neutral about whether
      the position was right, and all four forms above score zero against it. Building it
      requires parsing the reports for their recommendations as well as the responses. It is
      the next substantial piece of work on this project, and it is not published yet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2 id="backlog-chart" class="measure">The backlog, and why it is not the headline</h2>
    <p class="measure">
      This chart was on the home page until September 2026. It is here instead, because on
      its own it misleads. The line falls from {fmt(peak.backlog)} to {fmt(hist[hist.length - 1].backlog)}
      {" "}and a reader who goes no further takes away that the government has caught up. It
      has not. A report leaves this chart when a response is tabled, and the chart cannot see
      whether that response addressed the committee’s recommendations or closed the report
      without addressing any of them. Most of the fall was the second kind.
      {" "}<a href="#closures">How those responses are classified</a>. The chart is still
      published because the backlog is a real thing and this is the only long series of it
      that exists — but it is a measure of how much is outstanding, not of how much has been
      answered.
    </p>
    <BacklogChart points={hist} peak={peak} observed={snapshots()} />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="limits">Known limits and open questions</h2>
    <p id="acted-on">
      <strong>The largest limit, first: this measures answers, not outcomes.</strong> Every
      figure on this site is about whether a government responded to a committee, and none of
      them is about whether anything was done. A recommendation can be implemented without a
      response ever being tabled — through a budget measure, a regulation, an agency's own
      decision — and a recommendation can be answered warmly and never acted on. This site
      cannot tell those apart, and does not try. Closing that gap means reading budget papers,
      annual reports and estimates transcripts one recommendation at a time and forming a
      judgement, which is journalism rather than a register.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the record does establish is narrower and still worth establishing. The Senate has
      required a response within three months since 1973, the House within six since 2010, and
      a report with no response is a failure to account to the chamber that asked — an
      obligation owed to the Parliament, not discharged by acting quietly. Read every count
      here as a measure of that, and of nothing else.
      {" "}<a href="/recommendations#reading">The same caution, on the recommendations</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Which reports attract the three-month rule.</strong> The President’s report
      sets out what it leaves out: reports on bills whose recommendations could only be dealt
      with during debate on the bill, estimates and annual-report scrutiny, the Senate’s
      domestic committees, Selection of Bills, and — usually — Public Works. Some reports the
      President nonetheless lists appear to fall inside those categories. The two readings
      cannot both be right. We have written to the Senate Procedure Office asking which is
      correct and will publish the answer here. Until then the register follows the
      President’s report, because that is the Senate’s own statement of what is owed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The deadline is three calendar months, not ninety days.</strong> The Senate's
      1973 resolution says three months and the President's report says three months, and
      his own “Response provided within 3 months” column marks a 92-day response as within
      time. Ninety days is a shorter test than the Senate applies, and using it counted
      eleven responses late that the Senate counts on time. Every figure here is meant to
      be a floor, so the rule is read as the Senate reads it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Sitting days.</strong> A response can only be tabled when the chamber is sitting,
      and three months can contain a long adjournment. That is a fair objection to the
      compliance figure and we have not tested it against the sitting calendar. It can be
      bounded instead: allow twice the deadline and
      {" "}{(100 - comp.within180 * 100).toFixed(0)}% of responses still failed; allow a full
      year and {(100 - comp.within365 * 100).toFixed(0)}% still failed. An adjournment can
      explain part of the gap between three and six months. It cannot explain a median of
      {" "}{fmt(comp.medianDays)} days.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Compliance counts only responses that arrived.</strong> Reports still waiting are
      not in the denominator, so a report that is never answered never counts as a failure.
      The compliance rate therefore overstates performance, as does every other figure here.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Attributing a late response to a government</strong> can be done two ways — by who
      tabled the response, or by who was in office when the report was tabled — and they give
      different orderings. Both are published on the
      {" "}<a href="/senate/#compliance">Senate register</a>, side by side, because choosing one
      would be choosing a result.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Interim reports are marked, not excluded.</strong> A committee's own interim
      report is listed by both presiding officers as awaiting a response, so it is here —
      taking it out would be this site overruling the record it publishes. It is flagged
      instead, because a government that answers only the final report has not necessarily
      left anything unanswered, and a reader counting the backlog should be able to see how
      much of it is interim.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The report index is Senate-weighted.</strong> The harvest of committee reports
      is drawn from the Tabled Documents database and covers Senate committees far more
      completely than House committees. This does not affect the Senate register. It does
      mean that where a response answers a House report, this site can identify the response
      but often not the report behind it. That is why some report titles on the House
      register are not links: a wrong link is worse than none.
    </p>
    <p id="backlog">
      <strong>History before this site existed is reconstructed.</strong> The line on the
      history chart is recomputed from report and response dates each time the dataset is
      rebuilt, so it can move if a source record is corrected. Points marked
      {" "}<em>observed</em> are what the register showed on the day and are never recomputed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And the reconstruction is not the same as the record.</strong> The President of
      the Senate has printed the number outstanding nine times since June 2022, and those
      counts are the dots on the chart. They do not match the line. Before the 2024
      clear-out the reconstruction runs about sixty reports high; after it, twenty to thirty
      low; by mid-2026 they are two apart. The two count slightly different sets — his report
      excludes categories the response register contains, and includes joint committee
      reports it does not — so neither is a correction of the other, and the gap is the
      measure of what a reconstruction cannot see. This page used to say the line was "a
      floor, not an exact count". On the evidence of his own nine readings it is not
      reliably a floor, and the chart now shows both rather than asking anyone to take the
      reconstruction on trust.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Refresh.</strong> A scheduled job rebuilds the dataset every Tuesday morning:
      it re-reads both presiding officers' schedules, rebuilds both registers, removes any
      report the government has since answered, and commits the result, which republishes
      the site. If any step fails, nothing is committed and the previous build stays up.
      Every page shows the date of the data behind it. Between those runs the figures do not
      move, so a number quoted from this site on a Wednesday is the number it showed on the
      Tuesday. A response tabled in between will not appear until the next run.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If a row here is wrong, we want to know, and the fastest way to change it is to tell us.
      Write to <a href="mailto:corrections@toohardbasket.org.au">corrections@toohardbasket.org.au</a>{" "}
      with the row and the source. Corrections are made within 24 hours of being confirmed and
      listed in the {" "}<a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a>, alongside the dataset’s
      full change history, so the record of what this site said, and when it changed, stays
      public.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full dataset and the code that builds it are in the
      {" "}<a href="https://github.com/toohardbasket-lab/toohardbasket">public repository</a> —
      data under CC BY 4.0, code under the MIT licence. Every figure on this site is produced
      by that code from those files. Nothing is typed in by hand.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .intro { padding-bottom: 1rem; }
  h2 { margin-top: 2.75rem; }
  h3 { font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 2rem 0 0.5rem; }
  .note { font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--ink-muted); }
  ul, ol { padding-left: 1.1rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  li { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }

  ol.forms { list-style: decimal; padding-left: 1.4rem; margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  ol.forms li { margin-bottom: 1.4rem; }
  ol.forms p { margin: 0 0 0.25rem; }
  .form-name { font-weight: 600; }
  .form-what { color: var(--ink-secondary); font-size: 0.9375rem; }

  .tag {
    display: inline-block; margin-left: 0.5rem; padding: 0.05rem 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-muted);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); border-radius: 2px;
    vertical-align: 0.1em;
  }
  .tag.on { color: var(--series-1); border-color: var(--series-1); }

  table.register { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  table.register td { padding-right: 1rem; }
  table.narrow { max-width: 100%; }
</style>
